---
/**
 * /blog/best-baby-name-apps — the English hub. REBUILT 2026-08-20 to the
 * cluster standard after the German rework: every competitor statement is a
 * sourced literal quote from apps-en.ts, disclosure above the H1, no fixed
 * price, no "December 2014" (unverifiable), no all-names nickname or
 * popularity-trend claim (nicknames cover 56%; popularity_history is empty).
 *
 * This URL is indexed but nowhere in the top 20 for the head query, which
 * Baby Name Genius, NameHatch and AppRecs own. The realistic job of this page
 * is to serve the long tail and to be the hub the two long-tail pages link
 * back to. Do not chase the head term with length; 900-1,500 words is the
 * range that ranks in this category.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import DisclosureEn from '../../components/DisclosureEn.astro';
import StoreFactsEn from '../../components/StoreFactsEn.astro';
import WhyNamore from '../../components/WhyNamore.astro';
import ClusterFootEn from '../../components/ClusterFootEn.astro';
import { APPS_EN, VERIFIED_EN, APP_STORE_EN, PLAY_EN } from '../../data/apps-en';

const canonical = 'https://www.namore.app/blog/best-baby-name-apps';
const campaign = 'best_baby_name_apps_en';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What is the best baby name app for couples?',
    a: 'Several apps let both partners swipe and show you the mutual matches, including Namore, Baby Name Together, Nameberry, Baby Name Genius, BabyCenter and NameHatch. The differences sit around that: whether you need an account, whether there are ads, and what happens after the match. Namore needs no account, shows no ads, and adds a final round that turns your matches into a ranked shortlist.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Are baby name apps free?',
    a: 'All of them are free to try and each one funds itself differently. The listings of BabyName, Baby Name Together, Baby Name Genius and BabyCenter disclose advertising; NameHatch prices monthly or as a pay-once six-month plan; Nameberry starts you with 50 free swipes. Namore is free with 100 swipes per partner per rolling 24 hours, with no ads; a single one-time purchase unlocks unlimited swiping on both phones, priced per country in your app store.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is there a baby name app where neither partner sees the other one’s votes?',
    a: 'Yes, and not only ours. Baby Name Together’s Google Play listing says each parent swipes independently and privately, with only mutual likes revealed, and Nameberry says you see only the names you both love. Namore works the same way and adds a decision round after the match, a stage between the two partners that we did not find in the apps compared here.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need an account to use a baby name app?',
    a: 'Usually yes, in some form. Baby Name Together links accounts through email addresses and BabyCenter uses personal codes. Namore uses no account at all: one of you opens a session and the other joins with a six-letter code.',
  },
];

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@graph': [
    {
      '@type': 'Article',
      headline: 'Best baby name apps in 2026, compared',
      description:
        'Six baby name apps side by side, each quoted from its own store listing with source links and dates, plus what Namore does differently.',
      inLanguage: 'en',
      datePublished: '2026-08-15',
      dateModified: '2026-08-20',
      author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Julian Ziegler' },
      publisher: {
        '@type': 'Organization',
        name: 'Namore',
        logo: { '@type': 'ImageObject', url: 'https://www.namore.app/logo@2x.png' },
      },
      mainEntityOfPage: { '@type': 'WebPage', '@id': canonical },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'FAQPage',
      inLanguage: 'en',
      mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: f.q,
        acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
      })),
    },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Best baby name apps in 2026, compared | Namore"
  description="Six baby name apps side by side, each quoted from its own store listing with source links and dates, plus what Namore does differently."
  canonical={canonical}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <article class="post">
    <DisclosureEn />

    <header class="post-head">
      <p class="post-kicker">App comparison · Retrieved {VERIFIED_EN}</p>
      <h1>Best baby name apps in 2026</h1>
    </header>

    <p class="post-standfirst">
      At some point one of you says a name and the other pulls a face. Just for half a second. But
      the name is gone after that, not because it was bad, but because it is now stuck to that
      reaction. That is how most names die, and no database size fixes it.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you just want to browse names on the sofa, almost any of these apps will do. The
      differences start to matter at the question of what happens when you both like the same
      name, and what happens when you do not. Six apps, each in its own words, and after that,
      what we make of them.
    </p>

    <h2>The other apps, in their own words</h2>
    <p>
      We could tell you what the other apps can and cannot do. The problem: we sell one ourselves,
      and you have no reason to take our word for it. So here is what each vendor writes in its
      own listing, typed out, linked, dated. Where a listing says nothing on a point, we say that
      too. Our opinion comes afterwards.
    </p>
    {APPS_EN.map((a) => <StoreFactsEn app={a} compact />)}
    <p class="post-caveat">
      Trimmed to the four rows couples decide on. Every block links its listing as the source, where
      the ratings and AI statements sit in full.
    </p>

    <h2>And then there is ours</h2>
    <p>
      Namore exists because we spent months failing to pick a name ourselves. Not for lack of
      candidates; we had dozens. But after a few weeks we were no longer talking about names, we
      were talking about who had given in more often.
    </p>
    <p>
      So the app is built for a narrower job than "help me find names": two people deciding
      without getting in each other's way. If you mainly want to browse, you are better served
      elsewhere, and we say so below.
    </p>

    <figure class="shot">
      <img
        src="https://www.namore.app/blog-assets/02-private-by-design.webp"
        alt="Namore's swipe screen showing a single name card."
        width="900" height="1947" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
      />
      <figcaption>One card at a time, and nothing on screen that would give your partner's vote away.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <WhyNamore
      lang="en"
      heading="What the app actually does"
      only={['design', 'decision-round', 'search-add', 'no-repeats', 'no-account', 'privacy-price']}
    />

    <p class="post-caveat">
      Where Namore is weaker: it is new. On the App Store since May 2026, on Google Play since
      August 2026, with few ratings and no grown community. If you want the reassurance of an app
      that millions have used, BabyName's 21K ratings or BabyCenter's 7.1K are the safer pick, and
      if you want the deepest research library, Nameberry's editorial name content has no equal in
      this list.
    </p>

    <h2>What we would recommend</h2>
    <p>From here on it is opinion, and a vendor's opinion at that. Take it accordingly.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>You want the biggest user base and a decade of track record: <strong>BabyName</strong>, 21K ratings.</li>
      <li>You want a completely free app with no purchases at all: <strong>BabyCenter</strong>.</li>
      <li>You want the largest claimed name pool and no download: <strong>NameHatch</strong>, in the browser.</li>
      <li>You love reading about names as much as choosing one: <strong>Nameberry</strong>.</li>
      <li>You have twenty candidates, weeks of circular conversations, and need to actually decide: that is what we built <strong>Namore</strong> for.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      And the most honest advice we have: all of these are free to try. Install two and see which
      one your partner still opens after three days. That settles it more often than any feature
      list, including against us.
    </p>

    <p class="cta-row">
      <a class="btn btn-ink" href={APP_STORE_EN(campaign)}>Get Namore for iOS</a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={PLAY_EN(campaign)}>Get it on Google Play</a>
    </p>

    <h2>Common questions</h2>
    <dl class="faq">
      {faqs.map((f) => (
        <div class="faq-item">
          <dt>{f.q}</dt>
          <dd>{f.a}</dd>
        </div>
      ))}
    </dl>

    <ClusterFootEn current="best-baby-name-apps" />
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
